<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <parent>
        <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-clients</artifactId>
        <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
        <version>0.6.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    </parent>
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <name>Parallel Consumer - Proxy Client Ruby</name>
    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-client-ruby</artifactId>
    <packaging>pom</packaging>

    <!--
        Thin Maven wrapper over Bundler (Gemfile beside this file). The commands below run ONLY
        under -Dpc.foreignClients - see the foreign-clients profile in the clients aggregator for
        why opt-in is load-bearing. An ordinary `bin/build.sh -am` therefore builds this skeleton
        and runs no Ruby toolchain at all.

        THE TEST PHASE RUNS `rake`, NOT `rspec`, and the difference is deliberate: rake's default
        task is rubocop-then-spec, so the bug finder runs in the same lane as the specs and on a
        developer's box rather than only in CI. The clients matrix runs its own rubocop step on
        top; that is a redundancy, not the first place the check fires.

        THE SPECS NEED THE PROXY MODULE'S TEST JAR, AND THE PROFILE BELOW IS HOW MAVEN ORDERS IT.
        The conformance spec spawns the harness (TestModeMain, in parallel-consumer-proxy's test
        jar). An earlier note in this place said Maven could not order that for us and told the
        reader to build the proxy by hand first; it can, the same way the Go, Python and TypeScript
        modules already do it, and the clients matrix row - which cannot run a hand command - was
        red on exactly this. Locally the hand command still works and is still the fastest way:

            bin/build.sh -pl :parallel-consumer-proxy -am -DskipTests

        The spec fails with that command in its message rather than skipping, because a test that
        quietly does not run is not a passing test. Recorded as a wave finding in
        docs/inflight/clients/ruby.md (astubbs#242).
    -->
    <properties>
        <pc.foreign.skip>false</pc.foreign.skip>
        <pc.foreign.build.executable>bundle</pc.foreign.build.executable>
        <pc.foreign.build.args>install</pc.foreign.build.args>
        <pc.foreign.test.executable>bundle</pc.foreign.test.executable>
        <pc.foreign.test.args>exec rake</pc.foreign.test.args>
    </properties>

    <profiles>
        <!--
            THE SIDECAR THE CONFORMANCE SPEC DRIVES, AND WHY IT IS A DEPENDENCY.

            spec/support/harness.rb spawns the real test-mode sidecar over the real wire, which
            needs the proxy module's test jar (TestModeMain and the harness) and its main jar.
            Declaring them here is what makes the CI row's own command build the proxy before this
            module's test phase; the row runs `package` rather than `test` for the other half of
            it, because the harness looks for jar FILES and `test` stops one phase short of
            producing them.

            SCOPED TO THIS PROFILE DELIBERATELY. No client depends on the engine, and with the
            profile inactive - every ordinary build - there is no dependency edge here at all, so a
            scoped build still runs no Ruby toolchain and never reaches the engine. Modelled on the
            go-e2e-harness profile, which was written when the Go row failed this same way.
        -->
        <profile>
            <id>ruby-e2e-harness</id>
            <activation>
                <property>
                    <name>pc.foreignClients</name>
                </property>
            </activation>
            <dependencies>
                <dependency>
                    <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
                    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy</artifactId>
                    <version>${project.version}</version>
                    <scope>test</scope>
                </dependency>
                <dependency>
                    <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
                    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy</artifactId>
                    <version>${project.version}</version>
                    <type>test-jar</type>
                    <scope>test</scope>
                </dependency>
            </dependencies>
        </profile>
    </profiles>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.codehaus.mojo</groupId>
                <artifactId>exec-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>
